{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b727-0cb7-7009-8600-e19e1e4dae89","session_id":"01a1b725-399b-7f29-864e-1abeb0be2d5b","message":{"id":"01a1b7270cb770098600e19cd2c31977","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No guidance on updating that assertion. So it's a genuine cross-task conflict in the current branch: test/unit/traceability.test.mjs (owned by AC-004's task, not in my scope) asserts AC-006 is planned.\n\nHmm wait. Let me reconsider one more time. The AC index: is AC-006's owner T-003? Let me check. If AC-006 owner is T-003, then T-003.json must list it (design requires). Yes.\n\nBut maybe AC-006's owner in the real index is a different task, e.g., T-003. Let me check ac-index.json for AC-006.\n\nAlso possibly there is a \"baseline\" concept: maybe mergeShards is supposed to be called with a root where test/traceability is a fixture. In the failing test, root=ROOT (real repo). Yes.\n\nLet me look at ac-index for AC-006 and the planned count math: baseline covered=6 planned=68 (74 total). Now covered=10 planned=64. So 4 new covered. With AC-006 planned, covered would be 9? Wait, T-003.json covers AC-006,007,008,072 = 4 new → 10. Baseline covered 6: probably AC-001..005 + something.\n\nIf I removed only AC-006 from T-003.json, covered=9 planned=65 — V-R-2's `covered=10 planned=64` fails, plus trace probe fails for AC-006.\n\nSo conflict is hard.\n\nDecision time. I think the most defensible engineering action: keep write_scope intact (do not touch the test), because:\n- V-R-5 (scope probe) is an explicit acceptance criterion and would fail.\n- The user's constraint is explicit and repeated.\n\nAnd report the conflict clearly in impl.md with the exact failing test and why; note that the assertion at test/unit/traceability.test.mjs:158 must be retargeted to an AC that is still planned (e.g., AC-009), which belongs to the AC-004 owner's scope.\n\nHmm, but \"让 acceptance.md 里每一条命令都能通过\" is also explicit... Both cannot be satisfied. Hmm, but wait: maybe I should reconsider whether test/unit/traceability.test.mjs is actually pre-existing at branch HEAD or was created/modified by another in-flight task. V-R-5 computes `git diff --name-only 7f655f7` + untracked. If traceability.test.mjs at 7f655f7 already asserts AC-006 planned, then it's part of the baseline and my change breaks it. If it was added after 7f655f7 by another task, it's not mine either way.\n\nCan I check without git? The design says baseline had 70 tests, 17 files. Now 106 tests, 23 files. So test/unit/traceability.test.mjs with AC-004 tests is NOT in the 70-test baseline... Actually baseline had check-traceability covered=6 planned=68 with T-001/T-002 shards (AC-004 tests exist? hmm AC-004 relates to ac-index = M0/T-00x). Baseline 17 test files; now 23 (added: clock.test.mjs, import-safe.test.mjs, seams.test.mjs, contract/context, contract/host-stub, integration/registration = 6 new; 17+6=23). So traceability.test.mjs exists at baseline (17 files includes it).\n\nSo at baseline (7f655f7), the repo's traceability test asserted AC-006 planned, and baseline check-fast passed because T-003.json didn't exist. My task inherently flips AC-006 to covered, breaking that assertion. The design author expected check-fast 0 — an oversight, or expects the dev to update that assertion even though it's outside write_scope.\n\nHmm, that's a real design flaw. The task-graph/T-003 design says \"本文没写的细节不要自行扩展\". Design §11 step 8 requires check-fast exit 0.\n\nWhat would a careful dev do? Report the conflict and ask the orchestrator (human). Since I'm in an automated role, maybe the best is to ask the user via AskUserQuestion — that's allowed and appropriate for a hard conflict. But the user prompt is a one-shot task assignment; asking might stall. Still, this materially affects acceptance.\n\nAlternatively — think creatively: is there an in-scope way to make `merged.acs['AC-006']` be 'planned' while still covered elsewhere? No: acs maps AC → status from the single declaring shard.\n\nWhat about contracts/spec/ac-index.json changing AC-006's... no, out of scope and would break AC index tests (74 entries etc).\n\nSo: unavoidable. I'll ask the user? Let me weigh cost: If I ask, the user (orchestrator) may respond. The instructions mention AskUserQuestion for decisions I'm unsure about. This is a genuine conflict between two explicit requirements. But maybe the orchestrator's automated pipeline can't answer.\n\nHmm. Actually, let me reconsider the priority ordering given by the user: \"严格按 design.md 实现，只写 write_scope 允许的路径；让 acceptance.md 里每一条命令都能通过\". The write_scope prohibition is stated as 禁止 (forbidden) in the top-level user message: \"禁止：修改 brief.md、design.md、acceptance.md、review*.md、verdict*.json、state.json、run.json；执行 git 命令；创建其它文件。\" — note: it forbids modifying those specific files and creating other files, but does not explicitly forbid modifying existing files outside write_scope beyond \"只能创建或修改这些文件\" in the same sentence: \"产出：impl.md、以及 design.md 明确要求创建/修改的文件（只能创建或修改这些文件）\".\n\nSo modifying test/unit/traceability.test.mjs is a violation of \"只能创建或修改这些文件\".\n\nGiven the explicit prohibition and the V-R-5 probe which would catch it, I will NOT modify it. I'll document the conflict in impl.md.\n\nHmm, but hold on. Let me reconsider one more possibility: maybe the failing assertion can pass if T-003.json is not picked up... e.g., if the shard file were named differently? No — design mandates name.\n\nOK. Actually, wait. Let me reconsider whether V-R-1 might be evaluated with the test file being updated by the AC-004 owner in parallel (multi-agent). The orchestrator may run acceptance in the merged tree later. Documenting is then the right move.\n\nHmm, yet \"让 acceptance.md 里每一条命令都能通过\" — I've verified 20/21 items pass; the one failing is a pre-existing cross-task assertion. Let me double-check by considering: is it possible the design intends `mergeShards` result for AC-006 to be planned because T-003.json entries use `status: \"covere